\n\
After both resolve, Long Feng dies, Fatal Fissure's trigger goes on the stack, I earthbend 4 — target one of my lands becomes a 0/0 with haste and 4 +1/+1 counters = 4/4 haste.\n\
\n\
Wait, actually let me re-read Fatal Fissure: \"Choose target creature. When that creature dies this turn, you earthbend 4.\" So I target Long Feng, then when it dies this turn, I earthbend 4.\n\
\n\
Earthbend 4: Target land I control becomes a 0/0 creature with haste that's still a land, put 4 +1/+1 counters on it. When it dies or is exiled, return it to the battlefield tapped.\n\
\n\
That gives me a 4/4 haste creature. Nice.\n\
\n\
Also, Long Feng's death trigger: \"Whenever another creature you control or a land you control is put into a graveyard from the battlefield, put a +1/+1 counter on target creature you control.\" Long Feng dying itself doesn't trigger its own ability (it says \"another creature\"). So no trigger for opponent.\n\
\n\
But wait — should I consider the order? If I cast Fatal Fissure first, opponent could respond... they have 1 card in hand and 2 untapped lands (Forest + the 2/2 Swamp). They could have a pump spell or removal. But it's turn 4, they've been playing earthbend stuff. Risk is minimal.\n\
\n\
