\n\
Hmm, actually let me think about this more carefully. The earthbend effect is a continuous effect created by the resolution of the spell/ability: \"Target land you control becomes a 0/0 creature with haste that's still a land. Put X +1/+1 counters on it.\" The \"becomes a creature\" effect — is it indefinite duration? Looking at the reminder text: \"(Target land you control becomes a 0/0 creature with haste that's still a land. Put two +1/+1 counters on it. When it dies or is exiled, return it to the battlefield tapped.)\"\n\
\n\
The effect doesn't say \"until end of turn\", so the land is a creature indefinitely (as long as it has the counters, presumably — base 0/0 plus counters). When it dies, the \"when it dies\" trigger returns it to the battlefield tapped. New object: it's just a Swamp land, no longer a creature (the effect that made it a creature applied to the old object... actually, continuous effects that change characteristics apply to the permanent; when the permanent changes zones, it becomes a new object and the effect no longer applies).\n\
\n\
So killing an earthbent land permanently neutralizes it as a creature (it comes back as a plain tapped land). That's actually great value!\n\
\n\
Wait, but earlier in the game, the opponent's 3/3 earthbent Swamp — it was earthbent via... turn 1 Dai Li Indoctrination (earthbend 2 → 2 counters, so 2/2). Then turn 5 it was a 3/3 (Leaves from the Vine chapter II put a +1/+1 counter on it, making it 3/3). It's been attacking repeatedly.\n\
\n\
OK so with that understanding, blocking decisions:\n\
