\n\
Risk: tapped lands. Three tapped lands in an aggressive deck could be awkward but the fixing and late-game draw (which fuels the \"draw two cards\" synergies) is worth it. Actually, the sac-draw ability costing 4 plus tap is expensive but in late game it helps trigger Wolfbat etc.\n\
\n\
Alternatively, play just the two Boiling Rock Prisons (they make black, the main color) and keep 3 Islands. 12 Swamp, 3 Island, 2 pool = 17. Black sources 14, blue sources 3.\n\
\n\
Hmm, which is better? Airship Engine Room gives U and R. Red is only useful for Vindictive Warden's hybrid cost (which black can pay) and Warden's firebending gives red mana anyway. So red is nearly useless. Airship is basically an Island that enters tapped with a sac-draw upside. Replacing a basic Island with it: you lose the ability to cast turn-1 blue (Forecasting Fortune Teller, Otter-Penguin are 2-drops at {1}{U}... actually {1}{U} means turn 2). Turn 1 plays: Corrupt Court Official {1}{B}, Deadly Precision {B}, Azula {1}{B}, Fatal Fissure {1}{B}. No blue 1-drops. So a tapped blue land on turn 1 isn't disastrous, but on turn 2 playing a tapped land means you can't cast a 2-drop. That's a real cost.\n\
\n\
The card-draw sac ability synergizes well with the deck's \"draw your second card\" theme (Wolfbat x2, Foggy Swamp Hunters, Otter-Penguin, Messenger Hawk). Clue tokens also help (Sold Out, Messenger Hawk, Forecasting Fortune Teller, Lost Days). So late-game mana sinks that draw cards are valuable.\n\
\n\
I think playing all three dual lands is reasonable: the fixing is good, and the deck has enough card-draw synergy to value the sac abilities. The deck isn't hyper-aggressive; it's more of a midrange/value deck with discard, removal, and card draw.\n\
